  {
   "affects": [
    "power budget",
    "DC IR drop",
    "PDN"
   ],
   "area": "power",
   "id": "rails-without-voltage",
   "issue": "1 supply rail(s) without a known voltage (found by their name, a pin name such as VBUS, VSYS, VBAT, VDD, or a field): kisi needs the voltage to put their loads and sources into the power tree, the budget and the IR drop. Found by name: confirm that each one is a supply.",
   "items": [
    {
     "components": 2,
     "found_by": [
      "pin J4 VBUS"
     ],
     "ics": [],
     "rail": "USB_POWER",
     "write": "USB_POWER@<V>V where a PI_REG entry names it (regulator output or supply input)"
    }
   ],
   "priority": "medium",
   "where_to_find": "Schematic: the regulator, load switch or connector that feeds the rail and its output voltage (regulator datasheet / voltage setting, USB VBUS 5 V, battery nominal voltage). A rail behind a ferrite, a 0 Ω link or a shunt takes the voltage of the rail before it.",
   "write": {
    "example": {
     "name": "PI_REG",
     "ref": "U2",
     "source": {
      "document": "PMIC datasheet",
      "kind": "datasheet",
      "note": "VSYS follows VBUS while USB is present"
     },
     "value": "VBUS > VSYS@5V pass"
    },
    "note": "Write the voltage where the rail appears in PI_REG: as the output of the part that feeds it (`+5V > VSYS@5V buck 90%`) or as a supply input (`VBAT@3.7V input`). Behind a ferrite, 0 Ω link or shunt use a pass element (`+3V3 > IMU_VDD pass r=0.5`: the voltage carries over; items[].write suggests it when kisi finds such a part). Net names with spaces in double quotes (`\"/Power/VDD CORE\"@0.9V`). Without a power tree: PI_SOURCE `NAME=<V>V` on the source part. Nets that are not supplies: ignore.",
    "to": "fields[] – the rail with its voltage as NAME@<V>V in a PI_REG entry"
   }
  },
